---
<JournalArticle slug="dither-tool" title={title} description={description} shareImage={shareImage} shareAlt="An archer drawing his bow, rendered in glowing white particles against a starry sky" projectUrl="/experiments/dither-boil/" readTime="3 min read" projectLabel="Dither Boil — the image-to-loop tool" keywords={['AI-assisted creative coding','dither animation tool','creative coding','image to animation','designer builder India']}>
  <figure class="journal-media"><img src="/assets/experiments/dither-archer-hero.jpg" alt="An archer drawing his bow on a rocky ledge, rendered in glowing white particles against a starry black sky" width="2400" height="1350" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><figcaption>The source: the archer artwork I fed into the tool, extended to 16:9 in Figma.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="journal-copy">
    <p>Some vacation projects begin with a plan. This one began with me staring at an image and wondering how much character I could squeeze out of a very small number of pixels.</p>
    <p><strong>Dither Boil</strong> is a browser-based creative coding tool I built to turn an image or video clip into animated 1-bit grain. Drop in a file, pick a look, adjust the texture and motion, then export a still or a looping video. Here’s what it made from the archer above.</p>
  </section>
  <figure class="journal-media"><video controls playsinline preload="none" poster={poster} aria-label="A source image transformed into a looping dither animation"><source src="/assets/journal/diwali/dither-tool.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video><figcaption>The result: the same archer, run through Dither Boil as a moving, high-contrast dither loop.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="journal-copy">
    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <ol class="journal-steps">
      <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 16V4M7 9l5-5 5 5M4 16v4h16v-4"/></svg><div><b>Drop in a file</b><span>Any image or a short video clip.</span></div></li>
      <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="4" y="4" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5"/><rect x="13" y="4" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5"/><rect x="4" y="13" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5"/><rect x="13" y="13" width="7" height="7" rx="1.5"/></svg><div><b>Pick a preset</b><span>Boil, Ordered, Diffusion or Hard cut.</span></div></li>
      <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 7h10M18 7h2M4 17h4M12 17h8"/><circle cx="16" cy="7" r="2"/><circle cx="10" cy="17" r="2"/></svg><div><b>Shape the grain</b><span>Dot size, grain, contrast, glow and the colour pair.</span></div></li>
      <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 4v12M7 11l5 5 5-5M4 20h16"/></svg><div><b>Export</b><span>A PNG still or a looping video.</span></div></li>
    </ol>
    <dl class="journal-facts"><div><dt>What it is</dt><dd>A browser tool for making animated dither textures from images or video.</dd></div><div><dt>Named presets</dt><dd>Boil, Ordered, Diffusion and Hard cut.</dd></div><div><dt>Who made it</dt><dd>Shubham Shinde, designer and creative technologist in Bengaluru, India.</dd></div><div><dt>AI in the process</dt><dd>I wrote the concept prompt; AI built the tool. The dither effect itself runs in the browser.</dd></div></dl>
  </section>
  <figure class="journal-window"><a href="/experiments/dither-boil/" aria-label="Open Dither Boil"><span class="jw-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>studioshubham.vercel.app/experiments/dither-boil</span><b>Live tool</b></span><img src="/assets/experiments/dither-boil-ui.jpg" alt="The Dither Boil interface: the archer artwork boiling as white grain in the preview, with Presets, Pattern and Tone panels on the right" width="2400" height="1500" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a><figcaption><em>Inside the tool</em>The full tool. The preview on the left boils live at 25 fps; presets, pattern and tone controls sit on the right, and export lives top right.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="journal-copy">
    <h2>A tool for happy accidents</h2>
    <p>The named presets are a starting point, not a set of locked filters. Pattern, dot size, dash length, grain, cut-off, contrast, brightness and glow can all be adjusted. You can also change the colour pair, playback speed and loop duration. That means the same source can go from a soft, detailed texture to a much more graphic, restless one.</p>
    <p>I wrote the concept prompt and AI built the personalised tool from it. I set what the experience needed to do and shaped the presets and controls around the image-making part. Once it’s open, the dither effect itself runs in the browser with canvas rendering. It isn’t an AI image generator: it transforms the file you choose using the dither controls on the page. The video above is a showcase output from the tool.</p>
  </section>
  <figure class="journal-presets">
    <ul>
      <li><img src="/assets/experiments/dither-preset-boil.jpg" alt="The archer in the Boil preset: random dashes that reshuffle every frame" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span><b>Boil</b>Random dashes, every frame</span></li>
